# Step-by-step solution

```sh
astrona shell [[.Name]]

# 1. The backend lives in another cluster — use its context
kubectl config get-contexts
kubectl --context "$ASTRONA_LINK_BACKEND_CONTEXT" -n backend scale deploy/backend --replicas=2

# 2. Where is the backend, seen from this cluster?
BACKEND=$(kubectl get configmap astrona-links -o jsonpath='{.data.backend\.host}')
kubectl create job call-backend --image=curlimages/curl:8.11.1 -- curl -fsS "http://$BACKEND:30080/"
kubectl wait job/call-backend --for=condition=Complete --timeout=120s
kubectl logs job/call-backend

exit
astrona submit
```

Why it works: all kind clusters share one container network, so a pod here reaches the backend's node by its container name. The backend publishes a **NodePort** (30080) — a ClusterIP Service or a pod IP from the other cluster wouldn't be reachable.
